 [17592186551616 1080 "y=\\ln{\\left(C + \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2} \\right)}" 13194139535980 17592186045443 true] [17592186551616 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535980 17592186045443 true] [17592186551616 1083 #uuid "55f667be-d4d4-5b14-b0fd-af942503ac45" 13194139535980 17592186045443 true] [17592186551617 1080 "y=\\pm \\sqrt{C + \\frac{5 \\left(x - 4\\right)^{4}}{2}}" 13194139535980 17592186045443 true] [17592186551617 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535980 17592186045443 true] [17592186551617 1083 #uuid "58c86c6d-7619-5b7c-b416-96c1c1700e10" 13194139535980 17592186045443 true] [17592186551618 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551626 13194139535980 17592186045443 true] [17592186551618 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535980 17592186045443 true] [17592186551618 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y′=\\frac{4 \\left(x - 3\\right)^{2}}{y}.$ Assume $x-3>0.$ Take both sign branches on intervals where the radicand is positive and $y\\ne 0.$" 13194139535980 17592186045443 true] [17592186551618 1072 #uuid "63e2b730-0d9e-5375-bfbb-085f883671f1" 13194139535980 17592186045443 true] [17592186551618 1074 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y′ & =\\frac{4 \\left(x - 3\\right)^{2}}{y} \\\\ y\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =4 \\left(x - 3\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x & =\\int 4 \\left(x - 3\\right)^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\int 4 \\left(x - 3\\right)^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ \\frac{y^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{4 \\left(x - 3\\right)^{3}}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\frac{y^{2}}{2}=\\frac{4 \\left(x - 3\\right)^{3}}{3}+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y^2 & =\\frac{8 \\left(x - 3\\right)^{3}}{3}+2C \\\\ y & =\\pm\\sqrt{\\frac{8 \\left(x - 3\\right)^{3}}{3}+2C} \\\\ y & =\\pm\\sqrt{\\frac{8 \\left(x - 3\\right)^{3}}{3}+K}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=2C$ is an arbitrary real constant. Use either sign consistently on each interval where the radicand is positive." 13194139535980 17592186045443 true] [17592186551619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551628 13194139535980 17592186045443 true] [17592186551619 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535980 17592186045443 true] [17592186551619 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y′=- 4 e^{- 2 x - y}.$ Work on intervals where the logarithm argument is positive." 13194139535980 17592186045443 true] [17592186551619 1072 #uuid "682503a5-3ea4-50fd-b2dd-c4f6cf554fa9" 13194139535980 17592186045443 true] [17592186551619 1074 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y′ & =- 4 e^{- 2 x - y} \\\\ e^{y}\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =- 4 e^{- 2 x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int e^{y}\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x & =\\int - 4 e^{- 2 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ \\int e^{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\int - 4 e^{- 2 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ e^{y} & =2 e^{- 2 x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\ne^{y}=2 e^{- 2 x}+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\ln{\\left(C + 2 e^{- 2 x} \\right)}.\n$$\n\nThe logarithm argument must be positive on the solution interval; $C$ is an arbitrary real constant." 13194139535980 17592186045443 true] [17592186551620 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551643 13194139535980 17592186045443 true] [17592186551620 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535980 17592186045443 true] [17592186551620 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y′=\\frac{2 \\left(x + 4\\right)^{3}}{y}.$ Assume $x+4>0.$ Take both sign branches on intervals where the radicand is positive and $y\\ne 0.$" 13194139535980 17592186045443 true] [17592186551620 1072 #uuid "6fa1db63-f18e-583d-ab37-46117a1bf486" 13194139535980 17592186045443 true] [17592186551620 1074 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y′ & =\\frac{2 \\left(x + 4\\right)^{3}}{y} \\\\ y\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =2 \\left(x + 4\\right)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x & =\\int 2 \\left(x + 4\\right)^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\int 2 \\left(x + 4\\right)^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ \\frac{y^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{\\left(x + 4\\right)^{4}}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\frac{y^{2}}{2}=\\frac{\\left(x + 4\\right)^{4}}{2}+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y^2 & =\\left(x + 4\\right)^{4}+2C \\\\ y & =\\pm\\sqrt{\\left(x + 4\\right)^{4}+2C} \\\\ y & =\\pm\\sqrt{\\left(x + 4\\right)^{4}+K}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=2C$ is an arbitrary real constant. Use either sign consistently on each interval where the radicand is positive." 13194139535980 17592186045443 true] [17592186551621 1080 "y=C + \\left|{x + 7}\\right|^{\\frac{2}{3}}" 13194139535980 17592186045443 true] [17592186551621 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535980 17592186045443 true] [17592186551621 1083 #uuid "83105ce1-a627-537e-90fa-45472b86b72a" 13194139535980 17592186045443 true] [17592186551622 1080 "y=\\pm \\sqrt{C - \\frac{8 \\left(x - 3\\right)^{3}}{3}}" 13194139535980 17592186045443 true] [17592186551622 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535980 17592186045443 true] [17592186551622 1083 #uuid "8326b7c5-af1b-5b98-acb4-56a14bab405e" 13194139535980 17592186045443 true] [17592186551623 1075 #edb/ref 1759218655